; ======================================================================
;  qstubs_q.pi4 - Arduino UNO Q: the seams the Q does not have yet.
;
;  WHAT THIS FILE IS, AND WHAT IT IS NOT.
;
;  Anvil's capability model (Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi) is a RUN-TIME gate, not a
;  compile-time one, and that is deliberate: there is no CompilerIf on a
;  chip name anywhere in the core, and a command is compiled once for
;  every board and degrades through RequireCap(). The consequence for a
;  board that lacks a hardware group is exact and unavoidable: setting
;  #CAP_USB = 0 makes the `usb` command refuse in a whole sentence, but
;  the file Anvil/Core/usb_cmd.pbi still NAMES thirty-odd HwUsb*
;  procedures, and an undefined call is a hard compile error. So the
;  procedures must exist to link, even though the gate means not one of
;  them will ever be reached.
;
;  That is what this file is. Every procedure below is the honest
;  "absent" answer for a group the Q has no backend for, using the
;  portable vocabulary hal.pi4 fixes: -1 is a getter's bad-argument /
;  unavailable answer, 0 is a count or a false, and the error-text
;  procedures return a whole sentence rather than an empty string.
;
;  IT IS NOT A PRETEND BACKEND. None of these touches a register, none
;  claims a device, and every family they belong to is gated 0 in
;  board.pi4, so the operator gets RequireCap's refusal and never one of
;  these values. If a stub is ever reached it means a capability was
;  declared 1 without a backend behind it, which is the bug this
;  arrangement is designed to make loud rather than silent - so the
;  reachable ones say so in their answer.
;
;  WHEN A REAL BACKEND ARRIVES it replaces the matching block here and
;  the board's #CAP_ flag goes to 1 in the same commit. Nothing else
;  changes; that is the whole point of the seam.
;
;  This file also carries the SMALL PORTABLE PIECES the core needs that
;  happen to live in Pi-4 files today - the architectural timer wrappers,
;  and the screen/keyboard/wifi no-ops the shared line editor calls.
;  Those are marked separately below, because they are a different kind
;  of thing from a missing driver.
;
;  REQUIRES: qefi_q.pi4, qcon_q.pi4, memmap_q.pi4, hwtimer_q.pi4 first.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Constants the shared core expects a board to name.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

; #MON_STACK MOVED TO memmap_q.pi4 ON 2026-09-08. It is a memory-map
; constant and it belongs with the memory map; it was here only because
; this file was where the board's answers to the core lived. What forced
; the move is that HwMonStack() became part of the map seam - the core
; asks the board for the stack top the same way it asks for the monitor's
; extent - and the two Q proof harnesses (tools/qcore_emu_check.pi4 and
; ArduinoQ/Examples/Diagnostics/anvilqcoreproof.pi4) include memmap_q.pi4
; and NOT this file, so a map constant living here could not be answered
; by them. This signpost stays so nobody re-adds the line and ends up
; with two definitions.

; Banner() prints the console's base address. There is no MMIO UART in
; this console - it is the firmware's EFI_SIMPLE_TEXT_OUTPUT_PROTOCOL -
; so this names the protocol's own base instead, resolved at run time.
; Zero at compile time; Banner reads the live value through gQConOut.
#UART_BASE   = 0

; uptime.pi4 reads a boot-count cell that RaspberryPi4/Lib/safety.pi4
; maintains across resets. The Q has no such cell (its resets go through
; UEFI ResetSystem and its DRAM is not preserved), so the magic can never
; match and uptime.pi4 reports "not available" rather than a wrong number.
#SAFETY_COUNT_ADDR = $63AF0000     ; inside the nominal monitor data window
#SAFETY_MAGIC      = $00000000     ; can never match - the count is absent

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Globals the shared line editor and commands read, which live in
;  RaspberryPi4/Board/display_globals.pi4 on the Pi 4.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Global gMouseTried.i = 1     ; 1 = "already tried", so ReadLine never asks
Global gKbdH.i = 0           ; no USB HID keyboard handle on this board
Global gScreen.i = 0         ; no framebuffer: the probe found no GOP
Global gLastCR.i = 0         ; the line editor's CR/LF de-duplication state
Global gBootTicks.i = 0      ; CNTPCT at entry, set by Main()
Global gBase.i = 0           ; the `base` command's address base
Global gMbx.i = 0            ; no VideoCore mailbox on a Qualcomm part

; ======================================================================
;  PORTABLE PIECES - not stubs. Real implementations that happen to live
;  in Pi-4 files today.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Ticks / TickHz / TimerInit - the ARMv8 architectural timer.
;
;  This is genuinely architectural, not Pi-4 hardware: CNTPCT_EL0 and
;  CNTFRQ_EL0 are ARMv8-A system registers and the A53 has them exactly
;  as the A72 does. hwtimer_q.pi4 already reads them (RdCntpct/RdCntfrq)
;  and the core wants them under these three names, so these are three
;  one-line renames, not a reimplementation.
;
;  CNTFRQ on this board is 19.2 MHz, read at run time rather than baked -
;  confirmed on silicon by anvilqcoreproof.pi4, against the Pi 4's 54 MHz.
;  A board that baked the constant would be wrong here by a factor of 2.8.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Ticks()
  ProcedureReturn RdCntpct()
EndProcedure

Procedure.i TickHz()
  ProcedureReturn RdCntfrq()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  millis() - milliseconds since the counter's zero, under the name the
;  core uses.
;
;  A FOURTH ONE-LINE RENAME, and it arrived with the `fan` command's
;  tick, which needs a millisecond clock and must not name a board's
;  library to get one. HwUptimeMs() in hwtimer_q.pi4 already computes
;  exactly this and its own header says it is "the seam name the core
;  asks for"; the core in fact asks for `millis`, which is what
;  RaspberryPi4/Lib/timer.pi4 calls it, so this is the bridge between
;  the two spellings and there is still only one division.
;
;  ITS BEHAVIOUR ON A STOPPED COUNTER MATCHES THE PI 4's: zero, always,
;  rather than a plausible small number. Both sides of the seam
;  returning a constant means a fan tick on a dead clock never fires
;  rather than firing on every spin, which is the safe direction - the
;  duty simply stops being re-evaluated and the last one stands.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i millis()
  ProcedureReturn HwUptimeMs()
EndProcedure

Procedure.i TimerInit()
  ; The architectural counter is always running on an ARMv8-A core and
  ; UEFI has already been using it, so there is nothing to start. Report
  ; failure only if it reads back as stopped, which would make every
  ; timeout in the monitor meaningless and must not pass quietly.
  If RdCntfrq() <= 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The line editor's polled hooks. Anvil/Core/parse.pbi's ReadLine()
;  calls these on its inner spin so that a Pi 4 can service
;  a framebuffer console, a USB keyboard and a Wi-Fi console while it
;  waits for a byte. The Q has none of those three: the probe found no
;  GOP, no HID is bound to ConIn, and there is no radio backend. They are
;  no-ops here, and the two that RETURN a value return the "nothing"
;  answer the editor requires - 0 and -1 respectively. Returning anything
;  else feeds the line editor phantom keystrokes.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure ScreenPump()
EndProcedure

Procedure MouseTryAttach()
EndProcedure

Procedure MouseTick()
EndProcedure

; No touch surface is present on this board. The shared line editor calls
; the service hook unconditionally; the honest board-specific answer is a
; no-op, while #CAP_TOUCH remains zero and the ABI returns #SVC_ENOCAP.
Procedure TouchTick()
EndProcedure

Procedure.i KeyboardChar()
  ProcedureReturn 0            ; 0 = no character (parse.pbi:51)
EndProcedure

Procedure NetConsolePump()
EndProcedure

; A local byte cannot contend with a network-console session here: this
; board has no network backend and therefore no remote owner to release.
Procedure NetConsoleClaimLocal()
EndProcedure

; The prompt re-arms the network console's bound UDP port on every spin
; (parse.pi4) so that a network command which bound a port of its own
; does not leave the console deaf. There is no radio here.
Procedure NetConsoleRearm()
EndProcedure

; The prompt also asks, on every spin, whether a link-local address this
; board gave ITSELF has had to be surrendered - RFC 3927 section 2.5, a
; SECOND conflicting ARP inside ten seconds, which must end in the
; address being dropped rather than defended again. This board has no
; network backend at all, so it can never have taken one; the NAME still
; has to resolve, for the same reason TcpTick below does.
Procedure NetConsoleLlTick()
EndProcedure

; DHCP has no interface or lease to service while #CAP_NET remains zero.
Procedure NetDhcpTick()
EndProcedure

; The prompt also gives TCP a zero-slice poll on every spin, so a
; connection opened by `tcp connect` finishes its handshake and its
; retransmission timer runs while nobody is typing. This board has no
; network backend at all (#CAP_NET = 0 in its board file), so there is
; nothing to poll and the whole of TCP is compiled out of it - but the
; NAME still has to resolve, because Anvil's core is one set of bytes
; for every board and parse.pi4 is core.
Procedure TcpTick()
EndProcedure

; There is no framebuffer/banner on this board, so the shared prompt's
; animation service hook is an honest no-op.
Procedure ScreenBannerTick()
EndProcedure

; The portable prompt calls one display service boundary. The Q console
; has no animated banner, but still receives its normal pending output.
Procedure ScreenServiceTick()
  ScreenPump()
EndProcedure

Procedure.i NetConsoleGetc()
  ProcedureReturn -1           ; <0 = idle (parse.pi4:60)
EndProcedure

Procedure WifiLinkTick()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cache maintenance. memcmd.pi4 flushes a range before running code that
;  was just written into it, so the instruction fetch sees the new bytes.
;
;  This is NOT a no-op and must not become one: it is a correctness
;  requirement, and it is architectural AArch64 (dc cvau / ic ivau /
;  the barriers), not BCM2711. Anvil enters at EL1 on the Q with UEFI's
;  MMU and caches already on, and EL1 may execute these by default
;  (SCTLR_EL1.UCI); the loop is written the way the architecture's
;  cache-maintenance example is, reading the line size from CTR_EL0
;  rather than assuming 64 bytes.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  TWO THINGS HERE ARE COPIED DELIBERATELY FROM THE PI 4'S VERSION
;  (RaspberryPi4/Board/cache.pi4:48) BECAUSE BOTH ARE TRAPS ITS AUTHOR
;  ALREADY FELL INTO AND DOCUMENTED:
;
;    * The address crosses into the ASM block in a GLOBAL, never a local.
;      Inline assembly in this tree can only name `global_<lowercase>`;
;      there is no local-variable symbol to reference, and every ASM block
;      in RaspberryPi4/ and ArduinoQ/ does it this way.
;    * The line is rounded down with a SHIFT, not `lo & $FFFFFFC0`. That
;      mask is correct for a 32-bit address and silently catastrophic for
;      a 64-bit one - it clears the whole top half too, so a flush of
;      anything above 4 GB cleans lines four gigabytes away and looks
;      entirely normal in a listing. The Q has DRAM up to 0x13FFFFFFF, so
;      this is not hypothetical here the way it nearly was on the Pi 4.
;
;  64 bytes is the line size on the A53 for both L1I and L1D, the same as
;  the A72's, so the Pi 4's constant carries over unchanged.
Global gQFlushAddr.i

Procedure QCacheLine()
  ASM
    adrp x9, global_gqflushaddr
    add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_gqflushaddr
    ldr  x9, [x9]
    dc cvau, x9
    ic ivau, x9
  EndASM
EndProcedure

Procedure CacheFlushRange(lo.i, hi.i)
  Define a.i
  If hi < lo
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  a = (lo >> 6) << 6
  While a <= hi
    gQFlushAddr = a
    QCacheLine()
    a = a + 64
  Wend
  ASM
    dsb sy
    isb
  EndASM
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  ABSENT SEAMS - the honest "not on this board" answers.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Autoboot. RaspberryPi4/Board/cache.pi4 keeps an autoboot entry point in
;  a preserved memory cell. The Q has no preserved cell across a UEFI
;  cold reset, so there is no autoboot address to remember and Banner()
;  correctly reports none.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i AutoEntry()
  ProcedureReturn 0            ; 0 = no autoboot entry armed
EndProcedure

Procedure AutoSet(a.i)
  ; Nothing to store: there is nowhere on this board that survives the
  ; reset. Silence here would be the wrong answer, so the command that
  ; calls this says so - see CmdAuto's gate in board.pi4.
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  RunAt(a) - branch to a loaded payload. This one is REAL and it works:
;  the address is called through the same indirect-call shape every UEFI
;  call in this port uses. It does not come back unless the payload
;  returns, which is the Pi 4's contract too.
;
;  IT STANDS THE INPUT DISPATCHER DOWN FIRST, for the same reason
;  RaspberryPi4/Board/cache.pi4's RunAt does: a payload takes the whole
;  machine, and anything Anvil/Core/input_events.pbi is still holding
;  when it goes - a byte already queued, a key-down whose release will
;  never arrive - would be delivered to a line editor that is not
;  running and could execute a command the moment the payload handed the
;  prompt back. InputPayloadSuspend() cancels the queue and the captures
;  and bumps the focus generation, so an event produced before the jump
;  can no longer be popped after it.
;
;  THE TOUCH HALF OF THE PI 4's HANDOVER HAS NO COUNTERPART HERE and
;  none is faked. That board calls TouchKeyboardPayloadSuspend(), which
;  puts a soft keyboard away, gives back the console rows it reserved
;  and withdraws its hit rectangle before ending in the same
;  cancellation. The Q has no touch controller and no soft keyboard -
;  TouchTick() above is a no-op and #CAP_TOUCH is 0 - so there is no
;  layer to put away, and only the dispatcher itself is stood down.
;
;  AND NOTHING IS RESTORED AFTERWARDS, because nothing was suspended:
;  the cancellation is a one-shot that leaves a #AIE_CANCEL in the ring
;  and a new generation behind it. The Pi 4 has a Resume twin only
;  because its keyboard layer carries a sticky flag; this board has no
;  such flag to clear, and adding one to look symmetrical would be a
;  state nothing reads.
;
;  THE ETHERNET HALF HAS NO COUNTERPART EITHER: the Pi 4 quiesces GENET
;  around the jump because its receive DMA is a bus master writing into
;  DRAM on its own schedule. Anvil drives no network hardware on the Q
;  (#CAP_NET is 0 and the console pumps above are no-ops), so there is
;  no bus master of Anvil's to stop.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure RunAt(a.i)
  If a = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  InputPayloadSuspend()
  QCall(a, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Settings. THESE STUBS ARE GONE, and their absence is the change.
;
;  This file used to carry a SettingsSet that always returned 0 and a
;  SettingsSayWhyNot that said "this board has no settings store: Anvil
;  reaches no writable medium on the Arduino UNO Q yet". Both were true
;  and both are now false: the board builds Anvil/Core/settings.pbi, the
;  real store, over the HwFile* seam in ArduinoQ/Board/hw_file_q.unoq,
;  which reaches the EFI System Partition through the firmware's own file
;  service. SETTINGS.TXT lives at \EFInvil\SETTINGS.TXT and survives a
;  power cycle, which is what makes a persisted boot target possible here.
;
;  Left as a note rather than deleted, because a reader who remembers the
;  stubs should find out what replaced them rather than conclude the
;  settings support was lost in a merge.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  GPIO and I2C are NOT stubbed here any more.
;
;  Both groups now have REAL backends on this board and their #CAP_ flags
;  are 1 (board.pi4):
;      ArduinoQ/Lib/tlmm.unoq      + ArduinoQ/Board/hw_gpio_q.unoq
;      ArduinoQ/Lib/geni_i2c.unoq  + ArduinoQ/Board/hw_i2c_q.unoq
;
;  The stubs that used to sit here returned -1 from every getter and said
;  the Q had no pins at all. That was true of Anvil, and it was never true
;  of the silicon: the QCM2290 TLMM has 127 GPIO lines at $00500000 and
;  two of the six QUP serial engines are I2C masters with their protocol
;  firmware already loaded by the boot chain. Both are reachable by plain
;  MMIO from a UEFI application at EL1, which was measured rather than
;  assumed.
;
;  They are deleted rather than left behind an #If, because two
;  definitions of HwGpioCount() in one image is a duplicate-symbol error
;  waiting to be resolved in whichever order the includes happen to land.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MMC - #CAP_MMC = 0. The Q boots from eMMC through SDHCI-MSM v5 at
;  mmc@4744000, and Anvil has no driver for it. HwMmcCanWrite() is the
;  write gate hal.pi4 describes, and 0 here means `mmc write` refuses
;  loudly instead of pretending.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwMmcUp()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure
Procedure.i HwMmcInfo()
  ProcedureReturn 0            ; 0 = no card brought up
EndProcedure
Procedure.i HwMmcBlockSize()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure
Procedure.i HwMmcBlockCount()
  ProcedureReturn 0            ; 0 = unknown capacity
EndProcedure
Procedure.i HwMmcType()
  ProcedureReturn #HW_MMC_UNKNOWN
EndProcedure
Procedure.i HwMmcReadBlock(lba.i, *buf)
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure
Procedure.i HwMmcCanWrite()
  ProcedureReturn 0            ; the write gate: refuse, never pretend
EndProcedure
Procedure.i HwMmcWriteBlock(lba.i, *buf)
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure
Procedure.i HwMmcErrorText()
  ProcedureReturn "no SD or eMMC controller is driven on this board: the UNO Q's eMMC is behind a Qualcomm SDHCI-MSM controller Anvil has no driver for"
EndProcedure

