; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Boot handoff - #CAP_BOOT_EL1 = 0.
;
;  This one is absent for a reason worth writing down rather than a
;  missing driver. anvilqcoreproof.pi4 read CurrentEL on the board and
;  got EL1 - UEFI runs this application at EL1, not EL2. Dropping a
;  kernel to EL1 requires being AT EL2 to install one. On the Pi 4 Anvil
;  runs at EL2 and can; here it cannot, and no amount of code fixes that
;  from inside the image. #CAP_BOOT_EL1 = 0 is a statement about the
;  execution context, not about unwritten work.
;
;  HwFirmwareDtb has no answer either: UEFI passes the device tree as a
;  configuration table, not in x0, so the Pi 4's "the firmware put it in
;  x0 at entry" does not hold. Reading it out of the configuration table
;  is real future work; claiming a pointer we did not verify would be a
;  silent wrong answer, so this returns 0 and the command says so.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwFirmwareDtb()
  ProcedureReturn 0            ; 0 = no device tree pointer known
EndProcedure

Procedure HwBootToEl1(entry.i, dtb.i)
  ; Unreachable: #CAP_BOOT_EL1 = 0 gates every caller. If it is ever
  ; reached, a capability was declared without a backend, and saying so
  ; is far better than branching somewhere with the wrong exception level.
  PrintN("!! HwBootToEl1 was reached on a board that declares #CAP_BOOT_EL1 = 0.")
  PrintN("   That is a bug in this board's capability declaration, not in the")
  PrintN("   command. Nothing was done - Anvil runs at EL1 here (UEFI put it")
  PrintN("   there), so it has no EL2 from which to install an EL1 for a kernel.")
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  HwMonLo() / HwMonHi() - where THIS monitor's image actually is.
;
;  THE CONSTANTS ABOVE ARE NOT THE ANSWER ON THIS BOARD, and the comment
;  on them has always said so: #MON_LO/#MON_HI name "a 1 MB
;  reserved-by-convention region" chosen so that a phase-1 proof could
;  exercise HitsMonitor() and get a host-predictable result. That was
;  honest for a run-and-report probe. It is not good enough for a
;  BOOTLOADER, because HitsMonitor() is the test that stops a payload
;  being placed on top of the running monitor - and against an invented
;  region it would have refused an address the monitor is not at, and
;  allowed the one it is.
;
;  UEFI knows. EFI_LOADED_IMAGE_PROTOCOL carries ImageBase and ImageSize
;  for this very image, and ArduinoQ/Board/qefi_q.unoq reads them off the
;  same structure it already resolves to reach the ESP.
;
;  WHEN THE FIRMWARE WILL NOT SAY, THE WHOLE OF DRAM IS REFUSED. That is
;  deliberate and it is the safe direction. A monitor that cannot find
;  out where it lives cannot prove a payload will miss it, and the
;  choices are to refuse everything or to allow everything. Refusing
;  everything makes `boot` stop working and say so; allowing everything
;  makes it work right up until it places an image over the monitor and
;  the board goes quiet. This project's whole philosophy picks the first.
;  It has never been observed - the protocol resolved on the first board
;  run - and the branch is here because "never observed" is not "cannot
;  happen".
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i HwMonLo()
  If QEfiImageKnown() <> 1
    ProcedureReturn #Q_DRAM_BASE
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn QEfiImageBase()
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwMonHi()
  If QEfiImageKnown() <> 1
    ProcedureReturn #Q_DRAM_TOP - 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn QEfiImageBase() + QEfiImageSize() - 1
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  HwPwm* / HwTemp* - #CAP_PWM IS 0 HERE, AND THIS IS WHY THERE IS NO
;  BACKEND RATHER THAN A WEAK ONE.
;
;  THE MODULATOR. The QCM2290 has PWM, and Anvil cannot reach it. The
;  general-purpose PWM on this part lives behind the same QUPv3 wrapper
;  that made #CAP_I2C zero - and that wrapper is the MEASURED hang: an
;  MMIO read of $04AC0004 stalled the interconnect and needed a physical
;  power cycle (board.pi4's #CAP_I2C note, 2026-09-02). The LPG/PWM in
;  the PMIC is further away still: it is reached over SPMI, and Anvil
;  has no SPMI controller. What is left is TLMM, which ArduinoQ/Lib/
;  tlmm.pi4 does drive - so a SOFTWARE toggle on a TLMM line would in
;  fact work. It is not offered, and that is a deliberate refusal rather
;  than an omission: this board's Arduino header belongs to the STM32U585
;  and not to the QCM2290 (see #CAP_GPIO), so the pins a fan could
;  plausibly be wired to are not the ones this software can reach, and
;  offering a modulator on thirty on-board control lines would be
;  offering to drive something soldered down.
;
;  THE THERMOMETER. The QCM2290 has a TSENS block. Anvil has no driver
;  for it, and unlike the modulator that is a plain missing driver
;  rather than a wall: TSENS is outside the QUP aperture and could be
;  brought up. It has not been.
;
;  So the pair the `fan` command needs is absent in both halves and the
;  capability is 0. THE DAY EITHER ARRIVES, this block is deleted, a
;  real backend replaces it and the flag goes to 1 in the same commit -
;  which is the whole point of the seam.
;
;  Every procedure below is the honest "absent" answer. None of them is
;  reachable while #CAP_PWM is 0: RequireCap refuses `fan` before the
;  first one, and FanBoot()'s own CompilerIf returns before the rest.
;  They exist so the image LINKS, which is the price of compiling one
;  command for every board instead of forking it - see the note at the
;  top of this file.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i HwPwmPinCount()
  ProcedureReturn 0            ; no pin here is offered for a fan
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwPwmPinAt(i.i)
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwPwmPinIsHard(pin.i)
  ProcedureReturn -1           ; not a pin this board will modulate
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwPwmDefaultPin()
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwPwmHzDefault()
  ; A frequency, not a zero, because FanBoot() reads this BEFORE its
  ; capability check and stores it as the frequency a future pin would
  ; use. Zero there would be a stored setting of zero hertz on a board
  ; that later grows a modulator. 25000 is the four-wire fan standard
  ; and is as good a placeholder as exists.
  ProcedureReturn 25000
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwPwmHzMin(pin.i)
  ProcedureReturn 0            ; 0 from both bounds means "no range"
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwPwmHzMax(pin.i)
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwPwmReservedReason(pin.i)
  ; 0 means "not reserved", which is true and is also not the reason
  ; `fan` refuses here - #CAP_PWM is. Answering with a sentence about
  ; the QUP wrapper would put a pin-specific excuse in front of a
  ; board-wide absence.
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwPwmBegin(pin.i, hz.i)
  ProcedureReturn #HW_PWM_PIN
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwPwmDuty(permille.i)
  ProcedureReturn #HW_PWM_STATE
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwPwmEnd()
  ProcedureReturn #HW_PWM_STATE
EndProcedure

Procedure HwPwmService()
  ; Reached on EVERY spin of the prompt through FanTick, and it must
  ; therefore be free. It is: FanTick returns on gFanArmed before
  ; getting here, and gFanArmed can only be set by a command RequireCap
  ; refuses. This body is the belt to that braces.
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwPwmKind()
  ProcedureReturn #HW_PWM_KIND_NONE
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwPwmPin()
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwPwmHz()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwPwmDutyNow()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwPwmDetail(which.i)
  ProcedureReturn -1           ; -1 is "this board has no such number"
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwTempMilliC()
  ProcedureReturn #HW_TEMP_NONE
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwTempMaxMilliC()
  ProcedureReturn #HW_TEMP_NONE
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwTempSource()
  ProcedureReturn #HW_TEMP_SRC_NONE
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE MAP SEAM - HwMonBytes / HwMonStack / HwMonRegion* / HwPay* /
;  HwStageAddr. NEW 2026-09-08.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The core stopped reading #PAY0_LO, #MON_DATA_LO, #MON_STACK and the
;  rest out of a board file that day, and it stopped because of the Pi 4:
;  the ruling is that Anvil is a kernel and may be any size, so the Pi 4's
;  low payload window begins above whatever the image turned out to be
;  and there is no constant to read. Once one board's map is arithmetic,
;  every board has to be asked rather than read - which is the same
;  argument that moved HwMonLo()/HwMonHi() here in the first place, and
;  this board is the one that made THAT argument, because UEFI puts this
;  image wherever it likes.
;
;  THE Q'S ANSWERS ARE MOSTLY THE CONSTANTS ABOVE, and that is fine: a
;  seam exists so each board can answer however it can, not so that every
;  board has to compute something. What matters is that the core asks.
; ======================================================================

; HOW BIG THIS IMAGE IS. UEFI knows exactly - ImageSize on the loaded-image
; protocol - and qefi_q.pi4 already reads it for HwMonHi(). When the
; protocol did not resolve, HwMonLo()/HwMonHi() refuse the whole of DRAM
; (see their note above); the size that pair implies is reported here
; unchanged rather than second-guessed, because a monitor that cannot say
; where it is should not claim to know how big it is either.
Procedure.i HwMonBytes()
  ProcedureReturn HwMonHi() - HwMonLo() + 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwMonStack()
  ProcedureReturn #MON_STACK
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The regions this monitor owns. TWO here, against the Pi 4's three: the
;  image (from the firmware) and the nominal data window. There is no
;  autoboot record on this board - autoboot is armed in raw DRAM that
;  survives a reset, and this board's DRAM does not survive UEFI's
;  ResetSystem (see #SAFETY_MAGIC above, which is zero for the same
;  reason).
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwMonRegions()
  ProcedureReturn 2
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwMonRegionLo(i.i)
  Select i
    Case 0 : ProcedureReturn HwMonLo()
    Case 1 : ProcedureReturn #MON_DATA_LO
  EndSelect
  ; An index this board does not have: an empty range, which matches
  ; nothing. See the same answer in RaspberryPi4/Board/memmap.pi4.
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwMonRegionHi(i.i)
  Select i
    Case 0 : ProcedureReturn HwMonHi()
    Case 1 : ProcedureReturn #MON_DATA_HI
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure HwMonRegionSay(i.i)
  Select i
    Case 0
      Print("this monitor's image, as the firmware placed it")
    Case 1
      Print("the nominal data window")
    Default
      Print("a region this board does not have")
  EndSelect
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The payload windows. Constants on this board, and the reasoning is in
;  memmap_q.pi4 beside them: a UEFI application's buffers are placed by
;  the firmware anywhere in DRAM, so the windows span the whole confirmed
;  DRAM extent and the running image is carved back out of them by
;  HitsMonitor() through the region list above.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwPayWindows()
  ProcedureReturn 2
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwPayLo(i.i)
  Select i
    Case 0 : ProcedureReturn #PAY0_LO
    Case 1 : ProcedureReturn #PAY1_LO
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwPayHi(i.i)
  Select i
    Case 0 : ProcedureReturn #PAY0_HI
    Case 1 : ProcedureReturn #PAY1_HI
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Where a host should stage a file it is sending.
;
;  NOT #PAY0_LO, which is the base of DRAM and belongs to the firmware.
;  #Q_FREE_LO is the confirmed contiguous free block, measured off the
;  running board from /proc/iomem, and it is the only address on this
;  part this project can hand a host without guessing.
;
;  IF THE IMAGE ITSELF IS IN THAT BLOCK - UEFI is free to put it there -
;  the staging address moves above it, rounded up to a megabyte, for the
;  same reason and by the same arithmetic the Pi 4 uses. It is not a
;  special case bolted on: on both boards the staging address is the
;  first megabyte-aligned address that is clear of the running monitor.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwStageAddr()
  Define a.i
  a = #Q_FREE_LO
  If HwMonHi() >= a And HwMonLo() <= #Q_FREE_HI
    a = ((HwMonHi() + 1 + $000FFFFF) / $00100000) * $00100000
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn a
EndProcedure
